{
  "title": "Some ingredient section headers are stored as measurement-less ingredients instead of setting section",
  "description": "Ramekin supports ingredient sections (1,992 prod lines have section set), but some headers still land as standalone ingredient items with no measurements and section null. \
Example (an America's Test Kitchen burrito recipe in prod): items 'Chipotle Sour Cream' and 'Burritos' appear as ingredients ahead of their groups. \
Also seen: 'To serve:' (14), 'Assembly:' (7), and other colon-terminated labels. \
A rough prod count (2026-09-27) finds 118 measurement-less items ending in ':' and ~155 Title-Case measurement-less items without a section (some are real ingredients like 'Salt'). \
Sources: mostly Smitten Kitchen (97) and America's Test Kitchen (66), then NYT Cooking (14) and Whole30 (13).\n\
\n\
Besides rendering wrong, these show up as 'unknown' ingredients in the calorie estimate and would be parsed as shopping list items.\n\
\n\
Investigate the ATK and Smitten Kitchen extraction paths (JSON-LD recipeIngredient often inlines headers as plain strings) and promote header-like lines to the section of the following ingredients. \
Be conservative with Title-Case detection: require no quantity plus either a trailing colon or a match against the source's grouping structure. \
Expect intentional data/ diffs from make pipeline; commit them. \
Note that the current detect_section_header (ramekin-core/src/ingredient_parser/item/line_classifiers.rs) already recognizes short colon-terminated labels like 'To serve:' and 'Assembly:', so many of the 118 stored colon headers are likely stale rows from older imports that a parser fix won't touch. \
Recipe reads return the stored ingredients JSONB as-is (server/src/api/recipes/get.rs). \
Also add a one-off make target that re-runs the current parser's section detection over stored recipes and saves a new recipe version for each changed recipe, through the normal Diesel save path (no raw SQL, version history preserved). \
Dry-run it against the prod DB first and report the counts.",
  "status": "open",
  "priority": 3,
